selection has been identified and proven to majorly impact
vehicle fuel consumption and emissions [3], [4]. Therefore,
to further save fuel/energy for existing and future on-road
vehicles, including the connected and autonomous vehicles in
the future, it is significant to find fuel-efficient routes for them.
Finding the most fuel/energy-efficient routes for on-road vehicles is known as the eco-routing problem. Recently, Google
Maps App also started to provide eco-friendly routes for users
to choose from [5]. The emergence of vehicle data platforms
based on connected vehicle technologies enables the collection
of real-time driver behavior, traffic, and fuel consumption
data [6], [7]. Such data also provides rich information for
computing eco-friendly routes.
The conventional methods that solve eco-routing problem
mainly follow a two-stage process. 1) With estimated vehicle
trajectories or speed profiles, estimate the expected cost in
terms of fuel consumption/emissions for all road links through
microscopic fuel consumption models [8], [9], [10], [11].
2) Search for eco-routes by minimizing the expected cost
through optimization methods: shortest path [8], [9], [10]
or dynamic programming [11]. Unfortunately, one critical
fuel consumption impact factor, traffic signal information,
is not considered in these conventional methods. The frequent
vehicle accelerations and decelerations caused by traffic signals can affect vehicle trajectories and significantly increase
fuel consumption, especially in city driving [3], [4]. In one
study considering stochastic eco-routing for signalized traffic
network [12], the eco-routing problem is formulated as a
Markov Decision Process (MDP). The traffic signal status
is then included in the MDP state, and the state transition
probabilities are used to compute the expected fuel cost.
However, all the above-mentioned eco-routing methods [8],
[9], [10], [11], [12] have two major issues. First, the estimated
or predicted road-link fuel/energy consumption costs may not
be sufficiently accurate. The link-based costs are time-varying
and can even be nonstationary due to the real-world dynamic
traffic. Therefore, the estimated or predicted link cost for
the time being may not equal/reflect the actual link costs in
the future. Second, it is difficult to develop a microscopic
fuel/energy consumption model to accurately estimate or predict the instantaneous fuel/energy consumption in real-world
driving conditions for a vehicle. Although machine learning
based fuel consumption models [13], [14] have been developed
to predict a vehicle’s fuel/energy consumption, their accuracy

