and generalization ability to unseen traffic conditions are
limited by the training data. Besides, the existing data-driven
fuel consumption models [3], [4] often fail to include a comprehensive set of fuel/energy consumption impact factors [15].
Their training data are not large and diverse enough to cover
all typical driving conditions. Therefore, to improve the model
accuracy and generalization ability, these offline constructed
microscopic fuel consumption models [3], [4] need to have
online adaptation capabilities in the future [15].
This paper proposes a one-step solution based on reinforcement learning (RL) for efficient and accurate eco-routing.
Highlights of the proposed approach include: 1) We bypass the
vehicle trajectory prediction and fuel/energy consumption prediction. This is because the predicted values may be inaccurate
and may not reflect the reality. Instead, historical real-world
trajectories and measured instantaneous fuel consumption data
are collected from on-road vehicles and directly utilized to
train a model that predicts eco-routes via RL. Specifically,
through a connected vehicle data collection platform [6],
[7] or a mobile App, the historical real-world trajectories
can be sampled from the dynamic traffic environment. The
instantaneous fuel consumption data can be accessed from
the on-board diagnostics (OBD) ports of modern vehicles and
collected through the same platform or an App. II) To capture
the traffic signal effect on fuel consumption with MDP, the
explicit estimation of state transition probabilities is no longer
needed. Rather, the transition probabilities can be implicitly
captured through a RL method, Q-learning. Through RL,
the model that represents action value function and predicts
eco-routes can be directly trained using accessible data (i.e.,
historical trajectories, measured fuel consumption and traffic
data). There is no need to go through an intermediate step (i.e.,
predicting fuel consumption), which may induce additional
errors.
To design such a desired RL solution for eco-routing, three
technical challenges need to be tackled. First, how to train
a RL model to capture dynamic traffic environments. The
dynamic traffic environments that are inherently in temporary
sequence may lead to a catastrophic forgetting issue to the RL
model [16]. The model can only capture the environments that
are learned most recently and forgets about the environments
learned a while ago. Second, from the learning environment
aspect, the dynamic traffic networks are nonstationary environments with distribution shifts. The nonstationarity can make
the catastrophic forgetting issue occur more frequently and
jeopardize the model’s accuracy and generalization capability.
Third, for eco-routing applications in practice, it is necessary
and important for the model to be able to quickly adapt to new
traffic conditions. Therefore, the model needs to be updated
continuously online. However, a signalized traffic network is
usually very complex, and the corresponding MDP state space
is large, which costs the RL a long time to learn and adapt.
The above challenges can be tackled through metalearning [17], [18] and an efficient feature representation.
Meta-learning is a methodology for learning to learn.
It enables a model to capture the distributions of various
learning tasks. Therefore, only a small set of training data
samples and less computation are needed to complete a new

learning task in meta-learning, which is also called few-shot
learning [17], [18]. Meta-learning can help an eco-routing
model adapt to a new traffic environment quickly. Since
the eco-routing model covers different distributions of the
traffic environments through meta-learning, the catastrophic
forgetting issue can be properly addressed. Additionally, nonstationary environments can be considered as a sequence of
stationary tasks [19]. Since meta-learning can continuously
adapt to distribution changes across tasks [17], [18], it helps
the eco-routing model quickly adapt to nonstationary traffic
environments.
From the feature representation perspective, a computationally efficient representation is important and desired.
In conventional RL-based navigation and path finding algorithms, raw images/videos are usually directly utilized as input
features [20], [21], [22], [23], [24]. Accordingly, to abstract
the underlying link connectivity information from pixels,
complex deep neural network representations with many hidden layers, e.g., convolutional neural networks (CNN), are
always required. Meanwhile, such representation can cost a
long training time. Graph convolutional network (GCN) [25]
directly takes graph features as the input, in which the link
connectivity has already been explicitly abstracted by the
graph model. Fortunately, it is straightforward to represent a
traffic network with a graph. Therefore, compared to CNN
that inputs pixels, applying GCN saves many lower neural
network layers and induces much simpler model architecture
and less training time. Furthermore, compared to CNN, GCN
is a more easily generalizable representation for traffic network features. The GCN representation focuses on the logic
connectivity of road links. It does not include the redundant
specific spatial positions and orientations of road links as
CNN does. A well-trained GCN representation for a traffic
network can be more easily generalized to represent other
traffic networks at different geolocations but with the same
network topography. This will further save the training time
if we want to conduct re-training/ transfer learning on these
traffic networks based on some pre-trained models in practice.
Thus, that forms another theoretical reason why GCN is a
better feature representation candidate than CNN for ecorouting. Recently, lightweight GCNs have been successfully
applied for traffic prediction [26], [27], [28], [29], yet there has
been no work on solving eco-routing through GCN based RL.
Additionally, from the practical application aspect, it is necessary to formulate eco-routing as a dynamic multi-objective
MDP (MOMDP) problem. In reality, drivers have different
preferences/weights on saving travel time and fuel/energy
consumption. Therefore, it is necessary for the model to
explore optimal routes in terms of various preferences on
travel time and fuel consumption. In this study, the optimal
linear support (OLS) [30] framework is adopted, which can
effectively support multi-objective deep reinforcement and
works efficiently with meta-learning.
This paper proposes a graph convolutional network based
multi-objective meta-deep Q-learning (GM2 DQL) for ecorouting. GM2 DQL applies the GCN for feature representation, OLS for multi-objective solution, and meta-learning
for training. This paper has four major contributions.

