MA et al.: GRAPH CONVOLUTIONAL NETWORK BASED MULTI-OBJECTIVE META-DEEP Q-LEARNING FOR ECO-ROUTING

1) This work applies reinforcement learning to address the
eco-routing problem considering critical traffic signal information. 2) This study adopts deep Q-learning (DQL) based
meta-reinforcement learning for eco-routing. The proposed
method achieves fast one-shot adaptation in a new environment and efficiently tackles the catastrophic forgetting
and nonstationary environment issues. In addition to solving the multi-objective problems, the proposed method can
also be applied to generic DQL-based meta-reinforcement
learning that has a discrete action space. 3) We introduce
OLS as a suitable muti-objective framework for meta-learning.
With DQL-based meta-reinforcement learning, we develop a
multi-objective meta-deep Q-learning algorithm. 4) Through
comprehensive simulation experiments, it is verified that GCN
is an efficient feature representation for eco-routing. Both
GCN and meta-RL help generalize the model to unseen traffic
conditions.
This paper is organized as follows: Section II presents
related work, while Section III elaborates the details of
GM2 DQL. Section IV discusses the experiments conducted
and provides an analysis of the corresponding results analysis.
Finally, Section V concludes this study and suggests directions
for future research.
II. R ELATED W ORK
This section first reviews existing works related to
eco-routing from the perspective of problem formulation.
Next, we review methods related to our GM2 DQL design
from the perspective of the solution space. These include deep
reinforcement learning for navigation/routing, multi-objective
reinforcement learning, and meta-learning for deep reinforcement learning.
A. Eco-Routing
Many conventional eco-routing solutions use GPS-based
vehicle trajectories to estimate the trajectories/speed profiles
for each road link, which are important inputs for fuel
consumption models. To estimate fuel consumption, authors
of [31] characterized driving patterns by five features from
GPS data and input them into microscopic engine map models.
A measure called weighted fuel consumption factor for each
link was calculated and fed into a shortest path algorithm for
finding eco-routes. To estimate vehicle operational parameters
on energy/emission for eco-routing, authors of [9] combined
multiple sources of traffic information including GPS trajectories through a data fusion algorithm. Later, other authors
proposed a simulation-based framework [32] for eco-routing.
Link-based speed was estimated by a traffic assignment model
and was further used to estimate link fuel consumption.
The method used cruise speed to calculate fuel consumption
without considering vehicle accelerations and decelerations.
Therefore, the estimated fuel consumption cannot well reflect
reality. Some researchers attempted to consider both fuel
consumption and travel time in eco-routing and formulated it
as a constrained shortest-path problem [10]. Their algorithm is
to minimize travel time with fuel consumption as a constraint.
In [33], link-based emission was modeled as a nonlinear function with respect to link length and link travel time. With the

7325

estimated emission as a constraint, a traffic assignment model
was developed to solve eco-routing problems. All the above
solutions rely on the currently estimated fuel consumption
values, which might not reflect the future unseen dynamic
driving conditions.
Rather than estimating the current fuel/energy consumption,
machine learning has recently been adopted for fuel/energy
consumption modeling. The learned data-driven models were
used to predict second-by-second fuel/energy consumption
values for each vehicle [13], [14] in the near future. With
collected GPS trajectories and CO2 emissions, support vector
machine (SVM) was applied in [34] to build a CO2 emission
model, which was further utilized to search for eco-routes
using the k-shortest path algorithm. In [11], Gaussian mixture regression was utilized for fuel consumption modeling
considering real-world vehicle trajectories. Based on the fuel
consumption model, a travel-time constrained eco-routing
algorithm was developed to search for eco-routes. The authors
found that the shortest path, fastest path, and eco-route could
be different for a real-world traffic network. Authors of [14]
proposed a probabilistic Bayesian learning approach to predict
the energy consumption of electric vehicles. They accounted
for the uncertainty in link energy consumption and adopted linear programming to find eco-routes. Google Maps [5] recently
started to provide eco-routes for users to choose from. The fuel
consumptions of different candidate routes are estimated based
on the National Renewable Energy Laboratory (NREL) Route
Energy Prediction Model by taking the predicted traffic speeds
of each route into consideration.
However, previous eco-routing methods did not explicitly
consider the impacts of traffic signals except a 2015 study [12].
The authors proposed a stochastic eco-routing method for signalized traffic networks and formulated them as MDP. The link
length and traffic signal status were used to estimate vehicle
trajectories. The estimated trajectories were further used to
compute the state transition probabilities of MDP and predict
link fuel consumption through a fuel consumption model.
Eco-routes can then be obtained by minimizing the expected
fuel consumption constrained by travel time through dynamic
programming. Additionally, previous studies mostly focused
on finding eco-routes without paying adequate attention to the
accuracy of fuel consumption models.
B. Deep Reinforcement Learning for Navigation/Routing
Deep reinforcement learning (DRL) has strong feature
representation capability due to its deep neural network structure. DRL models do not require expensive labelled data
and are trained through the interactions between agent and
environment. Different variants of CNN-based DRL models
have been developed to solve navigation problems considering simulated 2D or 3D maze-like environments [20], [35],
[36]. In Neural SLAM [37], a RL agent with an external
memory implemented with long short-term memory (LSTM)
and convolution demonstrated the ability to represent the
occupancy map of a simulated 3D world. Authors of [38]
showed that semi-parametric topological memory, which was
based on CNN encoders, can help agent build a topological
map of the simulated environment. In [39], authors proposed

