IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON INTELLIGENT TRANSPORTATION SYSTEMS, VOL. 25, NO. 7, JULY 2024

imagination-augmented agents (I2As) based on LSTM and
a convolutional encoder. They showed that I2As can predict
the trajectories from a learned environment model. To tackle
the sparsity of the reward signals in such environments,
auxiliary tasks were used to train agents [40], [41], [42].
The simulated environments in the above research were
simple and unrealistic. More realistic navigation schemes
in simulated environments were proposed in [21] and [22].
To handle navigation problems with real-world image data
as the input through DRL, the DRL-based robot navigation
methods and the corresponding real-world training dataset
were proposed [23], [24], [43]. Since images/videos were
directly utilized as the input in these studies, the more realistic
the environment was, the more complex deep network architectures were required to capture and represent the features.
Furthermore, the vehicle routing problem (VRP), which is
the generalized travelling salesman problem, was addressed
through recurrent neural network (RNN)-based DRL in [44].
This work aimed at solving the classical NP-hard combinatorial problem in graph theory. Although VRP is different from
the eco-routing problem in a signalized traffic network, the
success in [44] shows the potential of applying RL to find
eco-routes.
C. Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning (MORL)
There are two main types of MORL based on the number of learned policies, i.e., single-policy approaches and
multi-policy approaches [45]. Single-policy approaches focus
on finding the best single policy for multiple objectives
with given/derived preferences. For example, the weighted
sum [46], [47] approach combines multi-objective functions.
The w-learning approach [48] assigns the optimal action for at
least one objective. [49], [50] quantify the degrees of importance for different objectives so that the optimal action can be
obtained through comparisons. The geometric approach [51]
computes the average reward for multiple objectives. Overall,
single-policy approaches require analysts to explicitly specify
their preferences/weights for different objectives and cannot
automatically explore all preferences.
On the other hand, multi-policy approaches aim at finding a set of policies that approximate the Pareto front. For
example, the convex hull approach [52] simultaneously learns
optimal policies for all linear preferences. A value-based
RL method extending the Bellman equation was proposed
in [53], [54] to maintain the convex hull of Pareto front.
In [55], a fitted Q-iteration (FQI) approach was developed
to approximate the Pareto front. However, these methods are
difficult to scale up with respect to the preference space,
and it is challenging for them to adapt to new preferences.
The scalarized Q-learning, i.e., OLS [30], embeds the deep
Q-learning algorithm inside an outer loop method. The outer
loop efficiently explores potential preferences in the convex
coverage set, and the inner loop conducts multi-policy updates
for the Q-functions scalarized by the preferences. As a result,
the complete optimal policies for all possible preferences
can be searched. In modular MODRL [56], decision values
are used to dynamically judge and adjust the priorities of

preferences based on scalarized actions. Obtaining the priority parameters requires extra computation. In [57], the OLS
was further extended to be based on a single pair of Deep
Q-Network (DQNs) to reduce computation. On top of these
works, to make the learning more sample efficient, authors
of [58] proposed a generalized MORL. A global optimality
filter was developed to update envelope Q-function. We have
identified that the original OLS is most suitable in our design
and works the best with meta-learning (refer to Section III-C
for details).
D. Meta-Learning for Deep Reinforcement Learning
Generally, there are three categories of meta-deep reinforcement learning. The first category is based on recurrent
neural networks (RNN) [59], [60], [61]. RNN, such as LSTM,
learns to learn a function that outputs the policy for an agent.
However, it is usually time-consuming to train such an RNN.
Later, model-agnostic meta-learning (MAML) [17] was
proposed as a generic meta-learning framework, and it can fit
any types of neural network models and learning, including
reinforcement learning. MAML was shown to outperform
RNN-based approaches in terms of computation complexity
and accuracy [18]. MAML was further extensively studied
from the perspective of gradient update [18], nonstationary tasks [19], [62], multi-agent environments [19], and
dynamic real-world environments [63]. It was demonstrated
that MAML-based methods can efficiently capture the distributions of tasks and rapidly adapt to a new task even with
limited exposure to the new task. Authors of [64] showed
that Q-learning was competitive among all types of RL when
applied to meta-learning. The MAML-based RL family [17],
[18], [19], [62], [64] belongs to the policy-gradient approach,
which is more suitable for addressing problems with a continuous action space. Therefore, they are not appropriate for
solving our eco-routing problem with a discrete action space
(cf. Section III. A for details).
The third category is the task inference-based approach,
in which an extra component that predicts future tasks is
introduced to improve the sample efficiency for the task.
In PERL [65], to interpolate off-policy learning, probabilistic
latent contexts for different tasks are explicitly modeled. The
model was used to infer tasks in an unsupervised manner.
In TESP [66], a task encoder based on RNN was developed
to generate latent task embeddings, which were further used
to sample episodes. POMDP [67] adopted a belief network for
task inference, which was trained through supervised learning
with privileged task information as the ground truth. Authors
of [68] proposed a self-supervised dual-agents reward shaping strategy for task exploration and a context-aware graph
network for task inference. Recently, TIGR [69] employed a
generative model and Gaussian mixture models with recurrent
units to capture the multi-modality of tasks. The evolvement
of the above inference-based methods suggests that achieving
high sample efficiency often requires task inference models
to be added to the meta-deep RL framework. To reduce
computation complexity, our design is based on the original
MAML-based methodology (cf. Section III-C for detailed

