works for the discrete action space.
III. G RAPH C ONVOLUTIONAL N ETWORK -BASED
M ULTI -O BJECTIVE M ETA -D EEP Q-L EARNING
To efficiently solve eco-routing problems through reinforcement learning, we exploit graph convolutional network
(GCN) representation, multi-objective reinforcement learning
framework, and meta-learning methodology, and develop a
GCN-based multi-objective meta-deep Q-learning (GM2 DQL)
algorithms.
With the commercially available connected vehicle-based
data acquisition and communication platforms [6], [7], it is
reasonable to assume that data transmissions are available
between vehicles and a data center. The GM2 DQL is achieved
through the interactions between a RL agent and a series of
learning environments that reflect the dynamics of the target
signalized traffic network. Every learning environment consists
of a graph representing the signalized traffic network, together
with its link-based costs (in terms of travel time and fuel
consumption) and traffic signal data. Specifically, link-based
costs are calculated by averaging the costs of all vehicles that
drive through a particular link over a short period of time. The
time-series traffic signal status during that period is logged as
the corresponding traffic signal data. The general process of
GM2 DQL is summarized as three steps: 1) In the remote data
center, GM2 DQL offline trains a GCN model that represents
the action value function used by the agent interacting with
the learning environments; 2) Vehicles requesting eco-routing
services download the model from the data center, and quickly
update the model to the latest local driving conditions online
with the most up-to-date learning environments (i.e., one-shot
learning); and 3) The updated models (i.e., adapted to the
local driving conditions) are applied by individual vehicles
to predict eco-routes.
A. Graphic Convolutional Network (GCN) Representation
for Markov Decision Processes on Multiple Road Links
To efficiently represent a signalized traffic network, the
eco-routing problem is formulated as dynamic multi-objective
Markov decision processes (MOMDP). The state, action and
reward are key elements that describe the MOMDP and
characterize the trajectories for eco-routing. In our preliminary
research [70], the state definition only contains the information
for a single road link. This definition assumes that each road
link is independent. In reality, the traffic conditions on road
links of a signalized traffic network can be causally related
with each other and not independent. Additionally, considering
multiple road links may help the agent capture the dependency
among road links and understand the overall traffic conditions
of the entire signalized traffic network. Doing so may make
the model more forward looking and generate eco-routes
that generalize well to unseen traffic conditions. Thus, it is
meaningful to relax the previous assumption in [70] and define
the MOMDP considering multiple road links.
We propose to apply GCN-based reinforcement learning
to capture the MOMDP on all links of the signalized traffic

7327

network. GCN-based RL takes the state of MOMDP as the
feature input and predicts the actions that will induce ecoroutes. The rest of this section defines the state, action, reward
of MOMDP for the GCN-based RL, followed by the GCN
model structure design.
1) State Definition: To make sure the state reflects multiple
road links as our new assumption requires, the connectivity
and characteristics of all links are needed. First, a graph is
needed to reflect the connectivity of the target traffic network.
The directed links of the traffic network are defined as vertices
of a graph, i.e., L = {L 1 , L 2 , . . . ,L N }, where N is the
total number of links. The corresponding adjacency matrix
A ∈ {0, 1} is utilized to represent the connectivity of the
links. If there exists a connection between two links L i and
L j , the corresponding entry in the matrix ai j = 1. Otherwise,
ai j = 0.
Second, the characteristics of all the road links are represented by a feature matrix X ∈ R N ×3 . In this paper, the feature
matrix X includes three specific feature vectors.
X = [0, T T , P]

(1)

0 ∈ R N ×1 is the traffic signal status vector that contains
the traffic signal status for all road links. When the traffic
signal for link i is green, vector element γi = 0. When the
traffic light is red, γi is the wait time, which is the remaining
red light/signal time for that link. Since the goal of this
specific work is to conduct proof-of-concept of our algorithms,
we assume the traffic signals on all the lanes of a link share
the same traffic signal status. Essentially, we are considering
a two-phase control. The state can be expanded to cover cases
with more complicated control strategies such as protected left
turn.
T T ∈ R N ×1 is the travel time vector of all the links. Each
element tt i is the vehicle travel time (in seconds) for link i.
P ∈ R N ×1 is the position information vector of the subject
vehicle. Its element pi is 1 if the subject vehicle is on link i.
Otherwise, pi is 0.
Lastly, the state is defined as the product of the preprocessed
adjacency matrix Â and feature matrix X , which is denoted
