2) Action Definition: An action is the decision of which
downstream link to be selected when the subject vehicle
is approaching the end of its current link. For a typical
intersection with four road-approaches possible actions are
turning left, turning right, and driving straight through the
intersection. Therefore, the action set A is defined as
A = {left, right, straight}.

(4)

3) Reward Definition: The reward r is defined as a vector
that contains the negative values of costs after an action is
taken. Since there are two objectives in the MOMDP, i.e.,
travel time (T T ) and fuel consumption (FC). The reward r
is:
r = [−T T, −FC]T .

(5)

In our case, rewards are mostly negative values. Once the agent
reaches the final destination, the reward will be maximum, i.e.,
0. In that sense, the agent will be rewarded for getting close
to the destination.
4) GCN Model Structure: The GCN model is used to represent the action value function for GCN-based reinforcement
learning. The GCN model takes the current state s as the input
features and predicts the best action a ∈ A as the output
that will lead to an eco-route. To represent the action value
function, both the GConv layers and the multi-layer perceptron
(MLP) layer are required. As mentioned in the state definition
section, stacked GConv layers can be utilized to capture the
complex nonlinear relationships among the features of all
connected road links of the signalized traffic network. An MLP
is used as the final output layer of the GCN model. Through
the MLP, the feature output from the GConv layers can be
mapped into the action space. For example, an action value
function can be represented by two GConv layers and one
MLP layer, which is written as
 

 
Q (s, a; θ ) = σ σ Âσ ÂX θ1 θ2 θ3
(6)
where θi denotes the model parameters for the i th layer, and
the ReLU function is selected for the activation function σ in
this work.
B. Data Collection and Preprocessing for Link-Based Costs
and Traffic Signals
Learning environments are required for an agent to learn
dynamic traffic of the target signalized traffic networks. As discussed in the introduction and related work sections, it is
important for the learning environments to accurately reflect
real-world dynamic traffic. For this purpose, the following
strategies are proposed to take advantage of the collected realworld data.
1) Definition of Graph-Based Learning Environment and
Link-Based Cost: In our design, a series of graphs are
generated from the real-world vehicle routing trajectories that are defined in the context of MOMDP, i.e.,
s1 , a1 , r 1 , s2 , a2 , r 2 , . . .}. The graphs proposed in this section

Fig. 1.

Free-flow segments and delay segments for a two-way link.

are the graph data structures used to efficiently represent the
learning environments. However, they are defined differently
from the graph applied for GCN representation in the previous
section (i.e., Section III. A). In these graphs, intersections
are defined as vertices and averaged link-based costs are
defined as edges. The costs for each graph contain the average travel time and average fuel consumption for all links.
These instantaneous costs from each vehicle are averaged
over all the vehicles that have traveled through each link in
a short period of time, e.g., 5-min. The average link-based
costs retain the cost variations over time that exist in raw
trajectories. They contain more important information than the
costs experienced by individual vehicles. Additionally, using
the average link-based data rather than raw trajectories helps
the model avoid overfitting during training, improve model
generalization, and save training time.
The link-based costs and traffic signal data for learning
environments are assumed to be collected through existing
commercial connected vehicle-based data platforms [6], [7],
and/or driver’s mobile devices. All the data is eventually
transmitted to the data center.
2) Traffic Signal Effect on Link-Based Cost: To reflect
the link cost variations due to the impacts of traffic signals,
we propose to split each road link into two segments: freeflow segment and delay segment. Heavily congested traffic
scenarios are not considered in this research. The free-flow
segment mainly reflects the average cost under the freeflow condition, which dominates the cost of a link when
the associated traffic signal is green. The delay segment
mainly reflects the average cost of delay at an intersection
when the red light is on. In this work, we define the delay
segment as the last portion of a link (e.g., last 25 meters of a
250-meter link), which ends at the center of the intersection
(illustrated in Fig. 1). The rest of the link is the free-flow
segment. Thereafter, the cost for each link can be represented
as
cost = cost link + cost delay .ratiodelay
