Authorized licensed use limited to: Sejong Univ. Downloaded on June 06,2026 at 00:51:56 UTC from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.

MA et al.: GRAPH CONVOLUTIONAL NETWORK BASED MULTI-OBJECTIVE META-DEEP Q-LEARNING FOR ECO-ROUTING

is used to further scale cost delay to estimate the delay for
a vehicle arriving in the middle of a red signal. If minor
traffic spillback happens when the traffic signal is green, traffic
spillback in the downstream of the segment causes the increase
of instantaneous cost values in the upstream free-flow segment.
Through averaging the instantaneous costs from individual
vehicles over a short period of time (e.g., 5-min), the effect of
minor spillback can be reflected in the cost link of downstream
free-flow segment. Therefore, we can still set the ratiodelay
as zero in this case and capture the major cost information.
3) Benefits of Graph-Based Learning Environment: There
are several advantages of applying such graph-based learning environments using real-world data. First, compared to
the simulation-based learning environments discussed in the
related works (Section II), our graph-based learning environments considering real-world data (traffic signal data, travel
time and fuel consumption data) can more accurately reflect
reality. Second, compared to directly utilizing actual costs that
are experienced/explored by an agent in the physical environment, the averaged linked-based cost reduces the complexity of
the learning environment, reduces training time, and enhances
model generalization. Conventionally, traffic flows of an urban
traffic network do not change much within a short period
(e.g., 5 mins), and the distribution of the costs over the traffic
network can be seen as a constant. Thus, it is unnecessary
to let the agent spend much time to explore the network
and learn the link cost distribution from the actual costs
experienced by individual vehicles. Meanwhile, doing this may
cause overfitting the model to the limited samples experienced
in a short period. Instead, the mean costs can be directly
obtained using our method, which saves tremendous learning
time and helps the GCN model generalize. Moreover, the GCN
model is essentially a function approximator. It can interpolate
and generalize to unseen traffic environments that might
cause variations to the cost distribution. Additionally, our
proposed meta-learning considers different cost distributions
learned from other environments in different time periods.
Moreover, the GCN model has the capability to interpolate the
small variations if major information about cost distribution is
provided. It will gradually complete the whole cost distribution
through reinforcement learning over time. Therefore, the mean
cost values from other environments/distributions can help the
GCN model further interpolate and generalize to previously
unseen situations.
C. Multi-Objective Meta-Reinforcement Learning
As discussed in the introduction, this study considers two
objectives in eco-routing. Therefore, there may exist multiple
optimal policies depending on the analyst’s preferences for
the two objectives. To address this issue, we adopt a specific
multi-objective reinforcement learning that is suitable for
meta-learning and propose multi-objective meta-reinforcement
learning algorithms in this research.
Recall that the state-of-the-art MORL methods (cf.
Section II-C) are based on DQN and the scalarized deep
Q-learning approach [30], [57], [58]. The objective of these
methods is to train an agent that can find the convex coverage
set (CCS), which contains optimal policies for all possible

7329

user preferences. The CCS theoretically is a subset of the
Pareto front for multi-objective optimization [57], [58], and
it contains all the optimal solutions with respect to different preferences for its outer convex boundary. The original
OLS [30] explores weight vectors and identifies whether a
weight vector belongs to CCS through the outer loop, and
the inner loop explores optimal multi-policies and updates
the scalarized Q-functions for a specific preference provided
by the outer loop. To improve the generalization ability of
OLS to preferences, generalized MORL algorithms [57], [58]
have been developed based on a single DQN model. These
generalized algorithms all share an additional step in the inner
loop, which trains the same DQN for multiple other possible
preferences.
We find the original OLS to be an appropriate MORL
solution and it works the best with meta-learning. The rationales are explained as follows. First, OLS can make the
meta-learning converge more easily. MORL trains DQN models over multiple preferences that are parameterized by a
weight vector w, and meta-learning conducts training over
multiple environments. Generalized MORL algorithms are
designed for a distribution of a single learning environment/
task. If a generalized MORL algorithm [57], [58] is adopted
together with meta-learning, the data samples from diverse
distributions are used in MORL, and they eventually randomize the only DQN model. For example, given samples from
N
N different distributions {si , ai , r i }i=1
that share the same
N
state and action, their rewards {r i }i=1 can be different or even
diversified. The optimal action for the next step ai+1 will also
be different.
R = w T r i + γ max w T Q (si+1 , ai+1 ; θ )
ai+1

(9)

Therefore, scalarizing rewards and optimal value vector
Q with different weights can eventually randomize the
Q-function that is represented by the only DQN model (i.e.,
θ ) during the gradient update of accumulated reward R. This
can further randomize the accumulated gradients of the DQN
model, so the optimization direction to the optimum is hard
to guarantee. Therefore, applying the OLS with multiple DQN
models dedicated for different weights can avoid such issues in
meta-learning and make the convergence more easily. Second,
regarding the concern that OLS might lack generalization,
the meta-learning can help generalize the models with data
from other distributions. Third, from the model adaptation
aspect, adapting the model for a given preference weight
vector is faster than adapting a model that covers all the
weights, because the training process with other weights will
not be executed. Therefore, it is worthwhile to spend additional
storage on multiple models for different weights to achieve
faster model adaptation speed.
The multi-objective meta-deep Q-learning algorithms are
proposed, which consist of two major parts: i) offline multiobjective meta-deep Q-learning with historical data in data
center (Algorithms 1 and 2); and ii) online model adaptation
with the most recent data on a vehicle (Algorithm 3). Fig. 2
illustrates the entire process of our proposed multi-objective
meta deep Q-learning algorithms for eco-routing.

