The multi-objective meta-deep Q-learning (Algorithms 1
and 2) runs offline in the data center using a large amount of
historical learning environments that are prepared as described
in Section III-B. During the offline learning, the OLS (in
Algorithm 1) is adopted as the outer loop and directs the
meta-learning function (Algorithm 2) to automatically explore
all the possible preferences parameterized by weight vectors
w}. To expediate the learning speed, a full model reuse transfer
learning strategy is applied when a new weight w ′ needs to
be explored. The complete DQN model parameters θ learned
from the previous w that is closest to w ′ in the sequence of
scalarization weights is reused. For the details of OLS, please
refer to [30].
The meta-deep Q learning (Algorithm 2) is developed and
set as the inner loop of Algorithm 1 to learn from multiple
environments for the given weight w. From the DQN parameters aspect, to reduce overestimation of actions [72], a double
DQN model containing an additional target network θ − that
shares the same structure as the DQN θ is adopted for gradient
updates.
The dynamic environments in Algorithm 2, denoted by envs,
are considered as a set of MOMDPs over time. Although
each environment may have its own cost/reward distribution,
together they can be seen as a joint distribution ρenvs . The goal
of meta-learning is to efficiently capture the joint distribution
ρenvs with a limited number of trajectory samples. For a
specific environment, the agent generates trajectories through
ϵ-greedy exploration (lines 8-16 in Algorithm 2). To improve
sample efficiency and model generalization, the trajectories
are stored in the replay memory D (line 12), so they will be
resampled differently and reused in the later learning iterations. For policy gradient-based RL that works for continuous

