As shown in Algorithm 3, a vehicle that requests eco-routing
service downloads the previously learned DQN models from
the data center (line 1 in Algorithm 3). The vehicle can quickly
adapt the downloaded models to the most recent learning
environment through regular deep Q-learning (line 3) and
achieve one-shot learning. To further accelerate the model
adaptation, adaptive ϵ-greedy exploration is adopted [73] in
this study.
To find the eco-route (Algorithm 4) based on the preference
weight w specified by the driver, the DQN model whose
weight vector is the closest to w is selected for eco-route
identification (lines 1-2 in Algorithm 4). Lastly, with the
model parameters θ frozen, the eco-routes are calculated using
lines 3-6 while the target vehicle is traveling through the
signalized traffic network. In this process, the actual cost of
the target vehicle is used.
IV. E XPERIMENTS AND R ESULTS
To evaluate the performance of our GM2 DQL method
for eco-routing, we conduct experiments that are based on
microscopic traffic simulation. The GM2 DQL algorithms are
implemented with the Python programming language and
PyTorch deep learning framework. The traffic environment
used to train and test the algorithms is simulated with a widely
used open-source traffic simulator called Simulation of Urban
Mobility (SUMO) [74].
A. Experiment Settings
As shown in Fig. 3, the signalized traffic network considered
for the experiments is selected from the City of Pasadena,
CA. It is sampled from the OpenStreetMap [75]. The traffic
network consists of 16 signalized intersections, 18 two-way
links and 6 one-way links. The mix of two-way and one-way
links and traffic signals at each intersection are very typical
for signalized traffic networks in major US cities.
With microscopic traffic simulation, randomness can be
introduced to train and test the proposed model’s ability to handle eco-routing in complicated and realistic traffic scenarios.
Given our scope defined in Section III-B, we need to configure
SUMO to generate sufficiently busy traffic conditions without

causing any congestion for this traffic network. To realize
this experiment using SUMO, 2,000 vehicles are simulated
to travel on the network in Fig. 3 over a two-hour period.
First of all, the trips of all the simulated vehicles are randomly
generated. This helps to randomize the trip origin-destination
(OD) pattern of the traffic network and decouple the OD
from any fixed distributions, allowing the trip OD pattern and
the distributions of costs on each link to change over time.
Compared to adapting to a traffic environment follows certain
OD patterns, random distributions significantly increase the
difficulty for the model to adapt. Therefore, the dynamic
random distributions are necessary and important to test if
the proposed GM2 DQL algorithms can quickly adapt the
model to a new traffic environment. Second, the cycle lengths
and phase durations for traffic signals are inherited from the
OpenStreetMap. The traffic signal timing plans of different
intersections are not coordinated, which also help quickly
diversify the distributions of OD and link-based costs. Third,
to thoroughly test our algorithms given the traffic network,
it is helpful to keep the target vehicle (i.e., the RL agent)
in the network as long as possible. Therefore, the agent will
make as many eco-routing predictions/decisions as possible.
Thus, the origin and destination of the target vehicle are set
as two locations that are separated by a maximum number of
links and the longest distance. Additionally, the learning and
testing environments are all sampled from the simulated traffic
by SUMO.
To achieve realistic simulations of an even larger traffic
network for model evaluation, besides the three key points
mentioned above, three additional aspects need to be properly
considered. One is to select a bigger traffic network with more
vehicles and trips. Second, dominant OD in addition to random
OD may need to be considered. Third, intersections with more
road-approaches (e.g., protected left turns) can be included.
Since a real-world implementation is not our goal in this paper
and it does not help us effectively evaluate the performance
of GM2 DQL, this can be the focus of our future research.
The HBEFA [24] model is adopted in this paper to simulate
the instantaneous fuel consumption of each vehicle. This
model is also included in SUMO. The architecture of the
model is configured empirically through experiments. The
GCN model in this paper is configured to have two GConv
layers with ReLU nonlinearities for each GConv, and an MLP

