Upper Right w = [0, 1], Lower Left w = [0.74, 0.26]).

B. Training Performance
The costs of each link, in terms of travel time and
fuel consumption, are calculated by moving average with a
5-min sliding window and a 1-min sliding step. Five learning
environments (i.e., link-based graphs) and the corresponding
traffic signal data are used for offline GM2 DQL training
(Algorithms 1 and 2). These five learning environments collectively reflect traffic conditions for 25 minutes. A separate set
of learning environment and its traffic signal data are collected
after another 5 minutes. This new dataset represents the most
recent traffic environment and is used for meta-adaptation
(Algorithm 3) training.
The offline training performance of GM2 DQL is shown in
Fig. 4. As can be seen from these plots, three preferences of
travel time and fuel consumption are automatically explored by
the algorithms and characterized by the following preference
weights (w): [1, 0], [0, 1] and [0.74, 0.26]. The increasing
trend of average reward per episode (ARPE) with respect to
training episode suggests the convergence of the GM2 DQL
(Algorithms 1 and 2). The ARPE for w = [0.74, 0.26] starts
with higher values since the training is automatically based
on the previously trained model with w = [1, 0]. Model with
weight w = [1, 0] is selected by the training algorithm since
the model’s weight is nearest to the target weight w = [0.74,
0.26] (line 6, Algorithm 1). In our previous study [70] considering a single road link each time for state representation,
the model training took about 15,000 episodes. In this paper,
considering multiple road links increases the state space and
consequently requires more meta-learning episodes.
1 One step of a trajectory is a s a , r sample.
t t t
2 Step here means training step, or gradient update.

Fig. 5. Training performance of meta-adaptation (Upper left: w = [1, 0],
upper right w = [0, 1], lower left w = [0.74, 0.26]).

The training performance of multi-objective metaadaptation (Algorithm 3) is shown in Fig. 5. Through this
step, the output DQN models from GM2 DQL (Algorithm 1)
are adapted to the latest environment within 650 episodes
for the model with w = [1, 0] and 500 episodes for the
models with two other weights. The adaptation episodes
are almost the same as in our previous study [70] using
MLP-based DQN models. The GCN-based meta-adaptation
also achieves online one-shot learning. Compared to learning
an environment from scratch, which takes about 5,000
episodes, the GCN-based meta-learning saves about 90%
computation time. However, compared to our previous
preliminary work that considers only single link in the state
representation [70], simultaneously considering multiple road
links increases the model adaptation time per episode. For the
implementation using single thread and only CPU (without
GPU), which reflects the worst case and slowest scenario,
the actual meta-adaptation time is about 30 seconds. This
is approximately three time longer than the model in our
preliminary study needs [70].
C. Testing for Eco-Routing
To test the model’s performance on predicting eco-routes,
the adapted DQN model is applied to a simulated target vehicle

Authorized licensed use limited to: Sejong Univ. Downloaded on June 06,2026 at 00:51:56 UTC from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.

MA et al.: GRAPH CONVOLUTIONAL NETWORK BASED MULTI-OBJECTIVE META-DEEP Q-LEARNING FOR ECO-ROUTING

that travels from the same origin to the same destination in new
dynamic driving conditions. To make sure the testing driving
conditions are different from the driving conditions used for
model training, we select the simulated traffic 5 minutes
after the last dataset used for meta-adaptation. To test the
generalization ability of the DQN models, multiple new traffic
environments are used, which are characterized by the traffic
data collected at 5 mins, 6 mins, . . . , 11 mins after the
latest meta-adaptation, respectively. It is meaningful to test the
models on future traffic conditions, since latency often exists in
collecting real-time traffic data. For example, the “real-time”
travel time information displayed on roadside dynamic message signs may be based on the data collected several minutes
ago due to latency. Furthermore, changes in traffic conditions
over a longer period (e.g., 15 mins) can be very significant
and beyond what the DQN models generalization ability can
handle. To avoid such risks, we recommend initiating a new
meta-adaptation 15 mins after the previous one. Therefore, the
new traffic environments 5 mins to 15 mins after the start of
a meta-adaptation are used for testing.
Evaluation Metrics Four metrics are used for evaluating the
proposed model’s eco-routing performance: tested travel time,
fuel consumption, travel time saving, and fuel saving. Since the
link cost distributions of various testing traffic conditions can
be very different, the travel time and fuel consumption of the
identified eco-route may also change significantly. Comparing
them across testing traffic conditions does not provide much
value. To address this issue, we propose another metric called
normalized cost as defined below:
tested cost-min
.
(10)
Normalized cost =
max − min
The normalized cost is based on min-max normalization,
and it reflects the relative distance of a route to the optimal
route with the minimum cost. The tested travel time and
fuel consumption values of an identified eco-route can be
normalized through equation (10) based on the maximum and
minimum costs of all possible paths. The range of normalized
costs is from 0 and 1. Value 0 indicates the identified eco-route
is optimal in terms of travel time or fuel consumption, and
1 means the route is the farthest from the optimal solution.
Usually, min-max is adopted for data normalization instead of
as an evaluation metric. This is because it can be affected by
outliers, which could be the samples from other distributions.
As discussed in the introduction, although the distributions of
each testing traffic environment are different, the distribution
of a specific environment can be seen as stationary and
fixed. Therefore, adopting the min-max normalization (i.e., the
normalized cost) as an evaluation metric is not an issue in our
case. Based on the five performance evaluation metrics, the
proposed eco-routing approach is compared with several other
benchmark routing methods.
Besides the GM2 DQL, three other eco-routing methods are also considered here for comparisons, which are
1) the Dijkstra’s shortest path method, 2) human driver’s
random exploration, and 3) our preliminary MLP-based metalearning [70] that only considers single link. As proposed
earlier in this section, simulated traffic 5 - 15 mins after

