the meta-adaptation are used for testing the four methods.
Specifically, the Dijkstra’s shortest path method is used to
find the fastest route and most fuel-efficient route. These
routes are calculated based on the traffic network in Fig. 3
with the latest traffic data received by a vehicle. The human
driver’s random exploration represents the use case without
considering any eco-routing method. It randomly explores
all the possible routes from the origin to destination that
consists of no more than 6 links. The 6-link constraint is
to avoid travelling backward. One of the randomly explored
routes is also the optimal eco-route. The MLP-based DQN
and GCN-based DQN models are tested directly under the
dynamic traffic environments according to Algorithm 4. For
these two DQN methods, three preference weights (i.e., w =
[1, 0], w = [0, 1] and w = [0.5, 0.5]) are selected to represent
typical drivers’ preferences: shortest travel time, lowest fuel
consumption, and considering both objectives equally.
Fig. 6 shows the testing results of the four methods based
on the traffic condition 5 mins after the last meta-adaptation.
Each point indicates the travel time and fuel consumption of
a route. As shown in Fig. 6 and TABLE II, both MLP-based
meta-learning and GCN-based meta-learning methods identify
the same eco-route for all the preference weights. GCN-based
method identifies the optimal route for travel time and/or fuel
consumption. MLP-based method achieves the same performance as GCN-based method in this case. The normalized
costs in TABLE II are calculated according to (10). The
results in TABLE II suggest that the GCN-based eco-route
takes 71% less travel time and saves 58% fuel compared to
the route generated by the shortest path method. The shortest
path method performs worse than the other methods, since
the traffic conditions change over the 5 mins. The eco-routes
identified through the shortest path method based on the traffic

