condition 5 mins earlier are not the optimal for the testing
environment 5 mins later. The shortest path method makes
decisions based on historical traffic information that is updated
every 5 mins. In other words, the identified shortest path
is optimal only in terms of the traffic information collected
5 mins ago. The simulated traffic environments are highly
dynamic in this study. Unlike the proposed reinforcement
learning model that can generalize to unseen traffic conditions,
the shortest path method therefore underperforms the proposed
GM2 DQL method.
The same kind of tests are also conducted with future
traffic environments. The testing environments are indexed
by the simulation start time, which are 6 min, 7 min, . . . ,
11 mins1F3 after receiving the latest traffic data and finishing
meta-adaptation. The evaluation results are shown in Fig. 7
through Fig. 12.
To more effectively present and evaluate the eco-routes
found by different methods in the tests, the normalized
costs of travel time and fuel consumption for all scenarios
(Fig. 6 – Fig. 12) are summarized in Fig. 13. The two plots in
Fig. 14 show the savings in travel time and fuel consumption
for all the tests. The horizontal axes in both Figs. 13 and 14
indicate the start time of different dynamic testing traffic
environments. The results shown in Fig. 13 and Fig. 14
also reflect the generalization performance of the proposed
model.
Overall, GCN-based meta-learning outperforms all other
methods, as can be seen from Fig. 6 – Fig. 14. The shortest path method tends to find routes with higher costs for
both travel time and fuel consumption, which indicates the
underlying traffic environment and cost distribution change
significantly even over short time periods and are nonstationary. Therefore, using instantaneous traffic information in
dynamic environments to find eco-routes often leads to suboptimal solutions. The previous MLP-based meta-learning
method can find the optimal routes in terms of both objectives with respect to all the preferences in the tests up
to 7 mins2F4 after the last meta-adaptation with real-time
traffic data (Fig. 6). After that, its eco-route finding performance becomes worse than that of the GCN-based method.
This shows the generalization ability of the MLP-based
