method is worse than the GCN-based method. Thus, considering multiple links in state representation through GCN
helps better generalize the model to unseen future traffic
conditions.
The shortest travel time route and most fuel-efficient route
are different in Fig. 8 and Fig. 9, which is caused by the major
changes in the underlying link cost distributions. In Fig. 8,
the GCN-based method finds an eco-route with normalized
fuel consumption being 0.05 and normalized travel time being
0.11 (Fig. 13). Based on the definition of normalized cost
in (10), these values suggest that the GCN-based method
performs well. The GCN-based method tends to select routes
that lead to shorter travel time for two reasons: 1) As discussed
in Section III, travel time information is considered in both
the state and reward definitions, while fuel consumption is
only considered in the reward. Therefore, the corresponding
GCN-based DQN model is better at differentiating travel time.
2) The differences among fuel consumption values are smaller
than travel time, although feature normalization is applied.
This makes it more difficult for the model to differentiate the
small differences in fuel consumption. Additionally, in Fig. 9,
the GCN-based method finds an eco-route with slightly higher
fuel consumption but much shorter travel time, compared
to the MLP-based method. This phenomenon can also be
explained by the above two reasons.
Fig. 13 and Fig. 14 show that the GCN-based DQN model
can generalize well and identify eco-routes within the next
15 mins, although traffic environments in this period are
not used to train the model. However, as shown in Fig. 13,
the fuel consumption and travel time of the routes predicted
by both the MLP-based and GCN-based DQN models have
an increasing trend over time. It suggests that their performances gradually deteriorate as time increases. This reveals

