the DQN model’s generalization ability is affected by the
nonstationary unseen future traffic. In practice, it is very
likely to have significant changes in urban traffic conditions
after every 5 to 15 mins. Our experiment results suggest that
meta-adaptation can handle unseen future traffic for up to
15 mins, beyond which the underlying link cost distribution
changes are too significant for the DQN model’s generalization
ability to tackle. In this case, another meta-adaptation would be
required.

GCN-based meta-learning can more accurately identify
eco-routes than the MLP-based meta-learning in [70]. The
reasons boil down to the fundamental differences between
these two state representation methods: 1) GCN representation
considers the spatial relationships among the features of
multiple road links of the entire signalized traffic network.
It is more suitable for representing complex MDP state space.
For the MLP-based method, if a feature of the current link
appeared before, this feature will not be considered as a
new state. In contrast, for GCN-based representation, any
feature change on any link generates a new state. Therefore,
GCN-based representation can describe a more complex MDP
with more states. This also explains why GCN-based metalearning requires more training time in both meta-learning
and meta-adaptation. Thus, multi-threading and GPU are recommended to further accelerate GCN-based meta-learning;
2) Since GCN-based meta-learning can handle/capture complex MDP states and the spatial relationships among road
links, GCN-based DQN models can be better generalized to
find eco-routes in unseen and dynamically changing traffic
conditions. Additionally, since multiple downstream road links
are considered by GCN-based DQN models, they are more
forward looking than the MLP-based approach that only
considers the current link in state representation. Overall,

Authorized licensed use limited to: Sejong Univ. Downloaded on June 06,2026 at 00:51:56 UTC from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.

7336

IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON INTELLIGENT TRANSPORTATION SYSTEMS, VOL. 25, NO. 7, JULY 2024

the results suggest that considering multiple links in state
representation helps to improve a model’s ability to generalize
and find eco-routes, and is more suitable for modeling complex
traffic networks/conditions (e.g., considering traffic signal status); and 3) From the model architecture perspective, despite
being more powerful, the GCN-based model may require
fewer model parameters than the MLP-based model. In our
experiment, the MLP-based model has 2,040 parameters.
While the GCN-based model only requires 540 parameters,
which is 3.8 times fewer. A simpler architecture can facilitate
model hardware implementation. Additional comparisons of
GCN- and MLP-based models are summarized in TABLE IV.
V. C ONCLUSION AND F UTURE W ORK
This paper proposes a GCN-based multi-objective metadeep Q-learning (GM2 DQL) method to efficiently learn a
dynamically changing signalized traffic network and automatically explore eco-routes in terms of drivers’ different
preferences of travel time and fuel consumption. The developed algorithms are evaluated using a signalized traffic
network simulated by SUMO. The results suggest that the
GM2 DQL method can quickly adapt its DQN models to
the latest traffic condition and achieve one-shot learning.
The adapted model can successfully identify eco-routes even
under future unseen traffic conditions (e.g., within the next
15 mins) that have never been used to train the model. Also,
the GM2 DQL eco-routing method can save up to 71% travel
time and 62% fuel consumption compared to the conventional
shortest path method. Compared to our previous MLP-based
eco-routing method that only considers the features of single
road link in state representation, the GCN-based method
proposed as representation part of the GM2 DQL can save up to
25% in travel time and 19% in fuel consumption. Additionally,
compared to the MLP-based method, the GCN-based method
requires nearly 4 times fewer model parameters, making it
a more computationally efficient representation of signalized
traffic networks. Therefore, the GM2 DQL is more suitable
for modeling/solving eco-routing in real-world complex traffic
environments.
Although it is tempting to evaluate the proposed algorithms and resulting model in the real world, doing so would
require a tremendous amount of resources (e.g., data from
connected vehicle platforms [6]) and is beyond the scope of
this research. For future work, the impacts of traffic network
size on the proposed model’s performance should be studied.
Different GCN model architectures (e.g., additional GConv
layers) can also be investigated. To scale our method to
larger traffic networks, we plan to follow through these two
action items: 1) It is anticipated that the size of the traffic
network will affect the model training and adaptation time.
We will identify the maximum traffic network size that can
be handled by the proposed model. 2) We will tackle the
eco-routing problem for a very large area by breaking down
the entire area into subnetworks. Moreover, this study has
considered randomly generated OD patterns and verified the
fundamental method. In the future, realistic time-dependent
OD matrices will be used to train and test our proposed
algorithms. Additionally, explicitly considering more realistic

traffic signal control schemes, such as protected left turns,
in MDP state representation may lead to additional interesting
findings. Finally, the fuel consumption model in this study
can be complemented by an energy consumption model for
electric vehicles, so that the proposed GM2 DQL method
can be extended for studying electric vehicle eco-routing
problems.
